1
00:01:12,855 --> 00:01:14,357
Better hurry.

2
00:01:26,185 --> 00:01:27,687
Here you go, my friend.

3
00:01:27,770 --> 00:01:30,272
Make sure you get us as close as you can!

4
00:01:30,398 --> 00:01:33,693
I'll give you something extra
if you make it a good trip.

5
00:01:33,776 --> 00:01:36,153
Hey, I'm going to get
you close, my friend,

6
00:01:36,237 --> 00:01:38,739
but not too close, eh?
You don't want to be eaten.

7
00:01:40,282 --> 00:01:41,492
Ready, <i>amigo</i>?

8
00:01:41,617 --> 00:01:42,868
Ready!

9
00:01:47,748 --> 00:01:50,793
One, two, three!

10
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:01,387
Eric, heave!

11
00:02:14,942 --> 00:02:16,235
Scared?

12
00:02:16,610 --> 00:02:18,154
Uh-uh. This is great.

13
00:02:31,125 --> 00:02:32,752
See anything yet?

14
00:02:32,960 --> 00:02:34,670
No, not yet.

15
00:03:04,784 --> 00:03:06,702
- What was that?
- I don't know.

16
00:03:10,831 --> 00:03:12,625
- Hold on!
- What's happening?

17
00:03:12,708 --> 00:03:13,876
Just hold on!

18
00:03:15,336 --> 00:03:16,962
Make it stop!

19
00:03:22,009 --> 00:03:23,511
What happened to them?

20
00:03:23,552 --> 00:03:27,640
I don't know.
Oh, my God.

21
00:03:27,973 --> 00:03:30,309
- We're going to crash!
- No, we're not.

22
00:03:31,852 --> 00:03:33,562
I'm going to cut this loose!

23
00:03:33,646 --> 00:03:35,648
- Let go of the rope!
- We're gonna crash!

24
00:03:37,483 --> 00:03:38,734
Move your hands!

25
00:03:39,819 --> 00:03:41,070
Come on!

26
00:03:43,823 --> 00:03:45,366
Here we go!

27
00:03:55,876 --> 00:03:57,419
It's going be okay, bud.

28
00:04:17,731 --> 00:04:19,567
Charlie, those are herbivores.

29
00:04:19,608 --> 00:04:21,652
They wouldn't fight each other.

30
00:04:21,735 --> 00:04:25,072
But these are carnivores, and they
really like fighting each other.

31
00:04:25,114 --> 00:04:29,034
They use their teeth and their claws
to rip each other's throats out.

32
00:04:29,702 --> 00:04:32,788
Alan, he's three.
Let's wait till he's five.

33
00:04:34,123 --> 00:04:35,666
Right. Sorry, Charlie.

34
00:04:35,749 --> 00:04:37,418
- Ellie?
- Yeah?

35
00:04:37,459 --> 00:04:40,296
It's Tom. He says he has to talk
to you about the last chapter.

36
00:04:40,379 --> 00:04:42,548
Tell him I won't lose the Jack Horner quote.

37
00:04:42,673 --> 00:04:44,884
My editor thinks he's a paleontologist.

38
00:04:45,718 --> 00:04:47,052
Hey, Mark!

39
00:04:47,136 --> 00:04:49,638
Hey, babe, look who's here.

40
00:04:51,307 --> 00:04:52,808
This is Alan.

41
00:04:53,142 --> 00:04:55,644
Nice to meet you, Alan.

42
00:04:55,728 --> 00:04:59,440
Daddy, this is a herbivore
and that's the dinosaur man.

43
00:04:59,481 --> 00:05:01,275
Dinosaur man?

44
00:05:01,817 --> 00:05:04,945
Jack, say my name.
Is my name Alan?

45
00:05:05,654 --> 00:05:07,281
Is my name Alan?

46
00:05:10,618 --> 00:05:12,036
He used to know me.

47
00:05:14,496 --> 00:05:16,665
- It's sad, huh?
- Here you go.

48
00:05:17,124 --> 00:05:20,336
Thanks. Mark's been working
for the State Department now.

49
00:05:20,419 --> 00:05:22,671
What do they do, Mark?

50
00:05:22,838 --> 00:05:25,341
International relations,
mostly treaty law, things like that.

51
00:05:26,634 --> 00:05:27,676
Uh-oh!

52
00:05:27,760 --> 00:05:29,637
- Call of the wild one.
- Here, I'll go.

53
00:05:29,845 --> 00:05:33,349
You guys catch up.

54
00:05:33,515 --> 00:05:35,017
Thanks, babe.

55
00:05:37,186 --> 00:05:38,854
He's a great guy.

56
00:05:40,272 --> 00:05:41,690
So, what are you working on now?

57
00:05:41,857 --> 00:05:43,275
Raptors, mostly.

58
00:05:44,109 --> 00:05:45,611
My favorite.

59
00:05:45,694 --> 00:05:47,947
Do you remember the sounds they made?

60
00:05:49,114 --> 00:05:50,658
I try not to.

61
00:05:50,699 --> 00:05:53,535
All our theories about raptor intelligence,

62
00:05:53,577 --> 00:05:56,413
what they were capable of,
we weren't even close.

63
00:05:56,497 --> 00:05:57,623
Tell me.

64
00:05:57,706 --> 00:05:59,500
We did cranial scans of a fossil skull

65
00:05:59,541 --> 00:06:02,795
and found what looks to be a very
sophisticated resonating chamber.

66
00:06:02,878 --> 00:06:05,005
Wait a second.
So we were right.

67
00:06:05,047 --> 00:06:06,548
They'd the ability to vocalize.

68
00:06:06,632 --> 00:06:08,634
It's the key to their social intelligence.

69
00:06:08,717 --> 00:06:10,886
Which explains why they
could work as a team.

70
00:06:10,970 --> 00:06:12,721
They'd coordinate attacks so their prey

71
00:06:12,805 --> 00:06:14,473
wouldn't know what was going on.

72
00:06:14,556 --> 00:06:16,016
They could talk to each other.

73
00:06:16,058 --> 00:06:18,185
To a degree we never imagined.

74
00:06:18,394 --> 00:06:20,813
Ellie, they were smart.

75
00:06:20,896 --> 00:06:23,732
They were smarter
than dolphins or whales.

76
00:06:24,400 --> 00:06:26,819
They were smarter than primates.

77
00:06:32,408 --> 00:06:36,745
I just wanted to say, if you ever need
help, sometimes you forget to ask.

78
00:06:37,830 --> 00:06:41,208
So you can call me.
Anything, anytime.

79
00:06:41,917 --> 00:06:43,335
Sure.

80
00:06:47,715 --> 00:06:49,425
You're still the best.

81
00:06:50,259 --> 00:06:51,677
I mean that.

82
00:06:53,095 --> 00:06:54,930
The last of my breed.

83
00:07:03,105 --> 00:07:07,651
<i>It is through the study of the anterior
chamber in multiple specimens</i>

84
00:07:07,735 --> 00:07:09,903
<i>that we can determine the correlation</i>

85
00:07:09,945 --> 00:07:12,406
<i>between the upper palate and the larynx.</i>

86
00:07:12,448 --> 00:07:15,617
<i>This lets us theorize,
theorize, mind you,</i>

87
00:07:15,659 --> 00:07:19,830
<i>that the raptor was capable
of sophisticated vocalizations</i>

88
00:07:19,913 --> 00:07:23,125
<i>which would have been a tremendous
evolutionary advantage.</i>

89
00:07:23,167 --> 00:07:27,629
<i>Raptors were fierce, intelligent
and socially sophisticated.</i>

90
00:07:27,755 --> 00:07:31,467
<i>They were able to hunt in numbers
and coordinate their efforts.</i>

91
00:07:32,634 --> 00:07:35,679
<i>Were it not for the cataclysmic
events which overtook them,</i>

92
00:07:35,763 --> 00:07:39,016
<i>it's entirely possible that raptors,
rather than humans,</i>

93
00:07:39,099 --> 00:07:42,561
<i>would have become the dominant
species on this planet.</i>

94
00:07:43,020 --> 00:07:45,272
<i>I hope this has been of interest.</i>

95
00:07:45,314 --> 00:07:48,484
<i>It certainly excites us as paleontologists.</i>

96
00:07:49,109 --> 00:07:51,612
<i>There is much more to discover.</i>

97
00:07:51,653 --> 00:07:56,116
<i>That is why we continue to need,
and ask for, your support.</i>

98
00:08:00,788 --> 00:08:02,164
<i>Thank you.</i>

99
00:08:12,007 --> 00:08:14,218
<i>Thank you very much, Dr. Grant.</i>

100
00:08:14,384 --> 00:08:16,845
<i>Now, does anyone have a question?</i>

101
00:08:19,681 --> 00:08:20,766
<i>Fine.</i>

102
00:08:20,849 --> 00:08:24,228
<i>Does anyone have a question that
does not relate to Jurassic Park?</i>

103
00:08:26,480 --> 00:08:29,191
<i>Or the incident in San Diego</i>

104
00:08:29,566 --> 00:08:31,860
<i>which I did not witness?</i>

105
00:08:34,530 --> 00:08:35,697
<i>Yes, sir?</i>

106
00:08:36,532 --> 00:08:38,784
<i>Your theory on raptors is good and all,</i>

107
00:08:38,909 --> 00:08:41,203
<i>but isn't all this conjecture kind of moot?</i>

108
00:08:41,245 --> 00:08:45,666
<i>Once the U.N. and Costa Rica and everyone
decide how to handle that second island,</i>

109
00:08:45,707 --> 00:08:48,544
<i>scientists will just go in
and look for themselves.</i>

110
00:08:48,585 --> 00:08:52,214
<i>Dinosaurs lived 65 million years ago.</i>

111
00:08:52,256 --> 00:08:56,426
<i>What is left of them
is fossilized in the rocks.</i>

112
00:08:56,510 --> 00:09:01,014
<i>And it is in the rock that real
scientists make real discoveries.</i>

113
00:09:01,056 --> 00:09:04,560
<i>What John Hammond and
InGen did at Jurassic Park</i>

114
00:09:04,852 --> 00:09:08,522
<i>is create genetically engineered
theme-park monsters.</i>

115
00:09:08,564 --> 00:09:11,191
<i>Nothing more and nothing less.</i>

116
00:09:11,233 --> 00:09:14,528
Are you saying you wouldn't
want to get onto Isla Sorna

117
00:09:14,570 --> 00:09:17,114
and study them if you had the chance?

118
00:09:17,197 --> 00:09:21,702
<i>No force on Earth or Heaven
could get me on that island.</i>

119
00:09:33,964 --> 00:09:35,883
What we got here, Nashy?

120
00:09:35,966 --> 00:09:38,802
Einhorn 20-millimeter,
gas-operated semi-automatic.

121
00:09:38,927 --> 00:09:42,890
10-shot, magazine fed, rotating
bolt recoil-reducing muzzle brake.

122
00:09:42,931 --> 00:09:44,600
High explosive...

123
00:09:52,149 --> 00:09:53,483
Udesky.

124
00:09:53,567 --> 00:09:55,652
<i>It's Paul Kirby. Just checking...</i>

125
00:09:55,736 --> 00:09:58,822
Yes, sir. We're good to go here.

126
00:09:59,573 --> 00:10:01,116
Matter of fact, I can lock things down

127
00:10:01,200 --> 00:10:03,410
just as soon as you drop me that payment.

128
00:10:06,622 --> 00:10:11,293
Well, that's right, two of the very
best men I could possibly find.

129
00:10:13,295 --> 00:10:15,589
No, sir, I haven't worked
with them personally,

130
00:10:15,631 --> 00:10:19,801
but believe me, sir, both of these men
come very highly recommended.

131
00:10:28,018 --> 00:10:29,311
<i>Are you all right?</i>

132
00:10:29,353 --> 00:10:30,979
Nothing to worry about, sir.

133
00:10:31,480 --> 00:10:33,649
It's gonna be a walk in the park.

134
00:10:42,866 --> 00:10:45,494
Billy, I don't think
I'm doing this right.

135
00:10:53,961 --> 00:10:56,046
Let's try the toothbrush.

136
00:10:58,340 --> 00:11:00,050
You've got to go slowly.

137
00:11:01,677 --> 00:11:03,804
Just take a little bit at a time.

138
00:11:03,845 --> 00:11:06,848
I can never tell what's
rock and what's bone.

139
00:11:07,224 --> 00:11:08,892
Technically, it's all rock.

140
00:11:09,017 --> 00:11:12,312
The calcium in the bones
is replaced during fossilization.

141
00:11:12,813 --> 00:11:15,190
But you can feel the difference. See?

142
00:11:15,857 --> 00:11:17,359
Rough,

143
00:11:18,193 --> 00:11:19,569
smooth.

144
00:11:20,362 --> 00:11:22,197
Rough, smooth.

145
00:11:29,454 --> 00:11:30,747
Dr. Grant.

146
00:11:31,707 --> 00:11:32,874
Mr. Brennan.

147
00:11:33,500 --> 00:11:35,294
So, how did it go?

148
00:11:35,836 --> 00:11:39,339
Well, it's not too late
to change your major, Billy.

149
00:11:39,881 --> 00:11:41,717
- That good, huh?
- Worse.

150
00:11:41,800 --> 00:11:43,385
We'll have to pack up in four weeks.

151
00:11:43,468 --> 00:11:44,845
Three.

152
00:11:45,095 --> 00:11:47,055
I had to rent some equipment.

153
00:11:47,222 --> 00:11:49,349
Come here.
I got to show you something.

154
00:11:49,391 --> 00:11:50,892
You like computers, right?

155
00:11:51,059 --> 00:11:52,561
I like the abacus, Billy.

156
00:11:53,186 --> 00:11:55,397
Meet the future of paleontology.

157
00:11:55,439 --> 00:11:57,733
It's a rapid prototyper.

158
00:11:58,400 --> 00:12:00,902
I enter in the scanned data
from the raptor skull.

159
00:12:00,944 --> 00:12:03,613
The computer breaks it down
into thousands of slices.

160
00:12:03,697 --> 00:12:07,576
And this thing sculpts it,
one layer at a time.

161
00:12:08,910 --> 00:12:10,078
It's done.

162
00:12:19,921 --> 00:12:23,091
I give you the resonating
chamber of a <i>Velociraptor</i>.

163
00:12:23,800 --> 00:12:25,302
Listen to this.

164
00:12:36,772 --> 00:12:38,148
Wow.

165
00:12:38,815 --> 00:12:41,109
This is brilliant, Billy.
Really, it is.

166
00:12:41,568 --> 00:12:43,779
Sad to say, it's just a little bit late.

167
00:12:43,904 --> 00:12:47,783
Dr. Grant? Paul Kirby, Kirby Enterprises.

168
00:12:48,575 --> 00:12:50,911
My card.
How you doing, Billy?

169
00:12:53,121 --> 00:12:54,956
What can I do for you, Mr. Kirby?

170
00:12:56,666 --> 00:12:59,169
First thing, I'm a great admirer of yours.

171
00:12:59,252 --> 00:13:02,339
And I have a proposition
I'd like to discuss with you.

172
00:13:02,422 --> 00:13:04,883
Would you have dinner with
my wife and me this evening?

173
00:13:04,966 --> 00:13:06,259
It'll be our treat.

174
00:13:06,301 --> 00:13:07,677
That'd be great but

175
00:13:07,928 --> 00:13:10,972
I'm tired, I've been traveling.
Maybe some other time.

176
00:13:11,098 --> 00:13:13,517
Believe me, this will be worth your while.

177
00:13:13,600 --> 00:13:14,684
We'd love to.

178
00:13:14,768 --> 00:13:17,187
Oh, terrific!
That's the spirit. Good.

179
00:13:17,270 --> 00:13:18,814
This evening then.

180
00:13:28,240 --> 00:13:29,533
Billy, thank you for coming.

181
00:13:29,616 --> 00:13:30,742
Dr. Grant, how are you?

182
00:13:30,826 --> 00:13:33,829
Amanda, this is Billy and Dr. Grant.

183
00:13:33,870 --> 00:13:35,122
Mrs. Kirby, how are you?

184
00:13:35,163 --> 00:13:36,665
What are you boys drinking?

185
00:13:38,250 --> 00:13:40,669
- Ice Pick, Catfish.
- Two, please, Cat.

186
00:13:40,752 --> 00:13:43,004
We've admired your work for years.

187
00:13:43,088 --> 00:13:44,840
Really. Truly inspiring.

188
00:13:44,923 --> 00:13:47,217
Amanda and I just love the outdoors.

189
00:13:47,300 --> 00:13:50,512
We've been on nearly every adventure
tour they can come up with:

190
00:13:50,595 --> 00:13:53,765
The Nile, Galápagos, K2.

191
00:13:53,849 --> 00:13:57,644
We even have two seats reserved
on the first commercial flight to the moon.

192
00:13:57,686 --> 00:14:00,021
For our wedding anniversary this year,

193
00:14:00,105 --> 00:14:02,065
we wanted to do something really special.

194
00:14:02,149 --> 00:14:04,568
- Something...
- Once in a lifetime.

195
00:14:04,860 --> 00:14:08,155
I've chartered an airplane
to fly us over Isla Sorna.

196
00:14:08,405 --> 00:14:10,574
And we'd like you to be our guide.

197
00:14:12,242 --> 00:14:14,244
There you go, guys.

198
00:14:16,204 --> 00:14:19,875
That's a very kind offer, Mr. Kirby,
but I'm a very, very busy man.

199
00:14:19,916 --> 00:14:23,211
I can recommend a couple of guys
who are really highly qualified...

200
00:14:23,253 --> 00:14:26,131
No, no. You're the best.

201
00:14:26,381 --> 00:14:27,883
You've seen these animals in the flesh.

202
00:14:27,924 --> 00:14:29,926
There's no one who comes close to you.

203
00:14:30,552 --> 00:14:34,389
You wouldn't be able to fly low enough
to see anything of any real interest.

204
00:14:34,431 --> 00:14:36,725
See, that's the interesting part,

205
00:14:36,766 --> 00:14:39,144
because we have permission to fly low.

206
00:14:39,227 --> 00:14:40,395
How low?

207
00:14:41,021 --> 00:14:44,900
I'm no aviation expert, but a heck
of a lot lower than anybody else.

208
00:14:44,941 --> 00:14:46,526
From what I understand,

209
00:14:46,568 --> 00:14:48,737
it's pretty much whatever we want.

210
00:14:49,738 --> 00:14:51,114
That's hard to believe.

211
00:14:51,239 --> 00:14:53,158
Through my business dealings,

212
00:14:53,241 --> 00:14:55,118
import-export, emerging markets,

213
00:14:55,202 --> 00:14:57,287
I've made a lot of friends in high places.

214
00:14:57,370 --> 00:15:00,123
In this case, the Costa Rican government.

215
00:15:00,749 --> 00:15:02,751
Dr. Grant, you have no idea

216
00:15:02,834 --> 00:15:05,420
how important it is that you come along.

217
00:15:05,462 --> 00:15:06,755
It would make all the difference.

218
00:15:06,838 --> 00:15:07,923
Mrs. Kirby, I...

219
00:15:07,964 --> 00:15:09,716
And, of course, we'd love to make

220
00:15:09,758 --> 00:15:11,760
a contribution to your research here.

221
00:15:12,636 --> 00:15:13,803
So,

222
00:15:15,639 --> 00:15:19,100
I could write all kinds of numbers
on this check, Dr. Grant.

223
00:15:19,809 --> 00:15:20,936
Tell me,

224
00:15:23,271 --> 00:15:25,357
what's it gonna take?

225
00:15:38,821 --> 00:15:41,699
Even with what I pay you,
couldn't you afford a better bag?

226
00:15:41,782 --> 00:15:42,992
No way.

227
00:15:43,892 --> 00:15:45,394
This one's lucky.

228
00:15:45,978 --> 00:15:49,606
Couple of years ago, some buddies
and I went hang gliding

229
00:15:49,690 --> 00:15:51,733
off these cliffs in New Zealand.

230
00:15:51,775 --> 00:15:56,738
And this big updraft came and swept
me right against the side. Wham!

231
00:15:56,780 --> 00:15:58,824
That does sound lucky.

232
00:15:58,907 --> 00:16:02,035
It was this strap alone that saved my life.

233
00:16:02,578 --> 00:16:04,580
Got hooked on a rock as I was falling.

234
00:16:04,663 --> 00:16:08,417
Reverse Darwinism.
Survival of the most idiotic.

235
00:16:10,752 --> 00:16:14,423
Alan, I want to thank you
for bringing me along.

236
00:16:14,506 --> 00:16:16,592
The bones will still be there
when we get back.

237
00:16:16,675 --> 00:16:19,178
That's the great thing about bones.
They never run away.

238
00:16:20,345 --> 00:16:24,057
And truth is, you got me into this.

239
00:16:25,475 --> 00:16:29,104
And I have no intention of being
on my own with these people.

240
00:16:32,524 --> 00:16:34,026
Don't get too excited.

241
00:16:34,109 --> 00:16:35,944
Chances are, we won't see a thing.

242
00:16:37,237 --> 00:16:38,614
Your turn to be nice.

243
00:16:39,364 --> 00:16:40,699
Wake me when we get there.

244
00:16:48,332 --> 00:16:50,459
So how do you know the Kirbys?

245
00:16:51,501 --> 00:16:53,295
Through our church.

246
00:17:13,357 --> 00:17:14,399
Alan.

247
00:17:14,900 --> 00:17:16,485
Alan!

248
00:17:17,736 --> 00:17:19,988
Wake up. We're almost there.

249
00:17:34,002 --> 00:17:38,090
<i>Unidentified aircraft approaching Isla Sorna,
this is San Juan Approach.</i>

250
00:17:38,173 --> 00:17:40,342
<i>You're flying in restricted airspace.</i>

251
00:17:40,425 --> 00:17:42,636
<i>Immediately turn to coordinates two...</i>

252
00:18:32,978 --> 00:18:34,896
My God, I had forgotten.

253
00:18:47,576 --> 00:18:49,828
We did it, honey.
We're here.

254
00:18:50,579 --> 00:18:53,498
Cooper! If you see anything, yell up!

255
00:18:53,582 --> 00:18:55,334
No, I thought I'd keep it to myself.

256
00:18:55,417 --> 00:18:58,462
I'm sorry. Everyone, if you
look out the left of the plane,

257
00:18:58,587 --> 00:19:01,256
you can see a whole herd of <i>Brachiosaurus</i>.

258
00:19:01,340 --> 00:19:04,926
In fact, you can see at the front
of the group the alpha male grazing there.

259
00:19:04,968 --> 00:19:08,055
Udesky? Nash? How about up front?
You guys see anything?

260
00:19:08,096 --> 00:19:09,514
Nothing yet, Mr. Kirby.

261
00:19:10,515 --> 00:19:13,518
Mrs. Kirby, look!
Out here, you'll see a group of...

262
00:19:13,602 --> 00:19:16,772
Mr. Kirby, we have a landing strip.
You want me to put her down?

263
00:19:16,855 --> 00:19:20,025
No! I told you, I want to circle first,
see the whole island.

264
00:19:20,108 --> 00:19:21,943
What do you mean?
You can't land!

265
00:19:21,985 --> 00:19:24,071
Hold on. I can explain...

266
00:19:24,112 --> 00:19:26,114
You cannot land on this island!

267
00:19:26,198 --> 00:19:28,450
- It will be fine.
- Don't worry, please.

268
00:19:28,950 --> 00:19:30,660
- Are you crazy?
- Sit down!

269
00:19:31,703 --> 00:19:33,955
Will you please sit down?

270
00:19:43,673 --> 00:19:47,219
Billy, tell me we didn't land.

271
00:19:47,969 --> 00:19:49,638
<i>Eric!</i>

272
00:19:51,807 --> 00:19:52,974
<i>Ben!</i>

273
00:19:53,016 --> 00:19:55,310
I think they're looking for someone.

274
00:19:57,187 --> 00:19:58,480
<i>Ben!</i>

275
00:20:05,987 --> 00:20:08,323
Dr. Grant, are you all right?

276
00:20:08,407 --> 00:20:09,825
I'm sorry we had to be so rough.

277
00:20:09,908 --> 00:20:11,493
Who hit me?

278
00:20:11,827 --> 00:20:13,829
That would be Cooper.

279
00:20:14,496 --> 00:20:15,705
What are they doing?

280
00:20:15,831 --> 00:20:18,083
Setting up a perimeter
to make the place safe.

281
00:20:18,166 --> 00:20:21,253
Trust me, on this island,
there's no such thing as safe!

282
00:20:21,336 --> 00:20:23,213
We have to get back on that plane!

283
00:20:23,338 --> 00:20:27,217
Tell your wife to stop making that noise!
That is a very, very bad idea!

284
00:20:27,300 --> 00:20:29,594
- Amanda!
- <i>Ben!</i>

285
00:20:29,678 --> 00:20:33,140
Honey, Dr. Grant said that's a bad idea.

286
00:20:33,515 --> 00:20:34,683
<i>What?</i>

287
00:20:34,724 --> 00:20:37,436
He says it's a bad idea!

288
00:20:37,936 --> 00:20:39,771
<i>What's a bad idea?</i>

289
00:20:41,857 --> 00:20:43,400
What was that?

290
00:20:44,234 --> 00:20:45,861
That's a <i>Tyrannosaurus</i>.

291
00:20:45,944 --> 00:20:48,864
I don't think so.
It sounds bigger.

292
00:20:52,492 --> 00:20:55,370
We have to leave!
We have to leave now!

293
00:20:55,996 --> 00:20:57,873
- Get on the plane!
- What's going on?

294
00:20:58,540 --> 00:21:00,375
Get on the plane!

295
00:21:04,087 --> 00:21:05,213
What about the other guy?

296
00:21:05,297 --> 00:21:06,631
Cooper's a professional.

297
00:21:06,715 --> 00:21:08,383
He can handle himself.

298
00:21:08,425 --> 00:21:10,844
Get on the plane! Hurry!

299
00:21:11,178 --> 00:21:12,471
It's all right.

300
00:21:24,399 --> 00:21:26,234
- Paul, we can't...
- It's all right.

301
00:21:26,318 --> 00:21:27,486
We'll circle the island.

302
00:21:27,777 --> 00:21:29,362
Give me a hand, Udesky!

303
00:21:48,215 --> 00:21:50,050
- Stop!
- What are you doing?

304
00:21:50,383 --> 00:21:51,510
That's Cooper!

305
00:21:52,886 --> 00:21:54,304
Stop.

306
00:21:54,387 --> 00:21:56,223
Come on, get out of the way!

307
00:21:57,140 --> 00:21:58,767
You know I can't stop this plane.

308
00:22:01,269 --> 00:22:02,562
Oh, my God!

309
00:22:04,439 --> 00:22:06,608
- Fuel cut-off!
- We're going down!

310
00:22:32,592 --> 00:22:33,927
You okay?

311
00:22:34,719 --> 00:22:36,638
We're okay up here.

312
00:22:37,055 --> 00:22:39,099
Everyone, just stay put.

313
00:22:39,516 --> 00:22:41,685
San Juan Approach.
Mayday, mayday!

314
00:22:41,768 --> 00:22:43,478
Who has the satellite phone?

315
00:22:44,020 --> 00:22:46,356
I do. I got it right here.

316
00:22:46,439 --> 00:22:49,109
I'm not getting anything.
The radio's gone.

317
00:22:57,784 --> 00:22:59,327
We haven't landed yet.

318
00:23:02,622 --> 00:23:05,458
- <i>All circuits are busy.</i>
- Damn it! Oh, man.

319
00:23:07,877 --> 00:23:08,962
What was that?

320
00:23:11,798 --> 00:23:13,550
Keep still! Keep still!

321
00:23:13,633 --> 00:23:15,927
Relax. What is it? What?

322
00:23:18,013 --> 00:23:20,056
- What is it?
- Hold it!

323
00:23:21,891 --> 00:23:23,351
Hang on, everyone!

324
00:23:43,788 --> 00:23:45,248
Help me!

325
00:24:45,934 --> 00:24:47,602
Mrs. Kirby, come back!

326
00:24:47,977 --> 00:24:49,396
No!

327
00:24:50,146 --> 00:24:51,189
Amanda!

328
00:25:37,193 --> 00:25:38,903
Follow me! This way!

329
00:25:47,454 --> 00:25:48,747
In here!

330
00:26:23,615 --> 00:26:25,200
I think we lost him.

331
00:26:30,246 --> 00:26:31,873
It's okay, it's dead.

332
00:26:34,083 --> 00:26:36,544
Nobody move a muscle.

333
00:27:37,146 --> 00:27:39,774
No, no! Please, don't!

334
00:27:39,816 --> 00:27:41,568
Stop, please.

335
00:27:42,861 --> 00:27:45,488
It's time you did some
explaining, Mr. Kirby.

336
00:27:46,239 --> 00:27:48,825
We called everyone.
We did everything we could.

337
00:27:48,908 --> 00:27:50,034
No one would help us.

338
00:27:50,118 --> 00:27:52,745
The Costa Rican government
said this is a no-fly zone.

339
00:27:52,787 --> 00:27:56,249
The U.S. Embassy,
that's our U.S. Embassy,

340
00:27:56,291 --> 00:27:59,460
told us we should accept the inevitable.
Can you believe that?

341
00:27:59,502 --> 00:28:01,504
You let a 12-year-old
go parasailing alone?

342
00:28:01,588 --> 00:28:02,630
No!

343
00:28:02,672 --> 00:28:04,424
He wasn't alone.
He was with a friend.

344
00:28:04,465 --> 00:28:06,259
Ben Hildebrand.

345
00:28:06,342 --> 00:28:08,678
Paul and I divorced over a year ago.

346
00:28:09,012 --> 00:28:10,471
All right, why me?

347
00:28:10,763 --> 00:28:13,182
He said we needed someone
who'd been on the island before.

348
00:28:13,266 --> 00:28:16,436
Yes, but I did not tell you
to kidnap somebody.

349
00:28:16,895 --> 00:28:19,480
I have never been on this island.

350
00:28:19,731 --> 00:28:21,941
Sure you have.
You wrote that book.

351
00:28:22,025 --> 00:28:26,487
That was Isla Nublar.
This is Isla Sorna, Site B.

352
00:28:26,571 --> 00:28:28,656
You mean there are two
islands with dinosaurs?

353
00:28:28,740 --> 00:28:30,116
Stay out of this.

354
00:28:30,158 --> 00:28:31,701
How long have they been missing?

355
00:28:32,035 --> 00:28:33,077
Eight weeks.

356
00:28:33,161 --> 00:28:34,746
Almost eight weeks now.

357
00:28:37,040 --> 00:28:40,376
Billy. We'll go back to the plane,
salvage what we can.

358
00:28:40,460 --> 00:28:41,794
Then we make for the coast.

359
00:28:41,836 --> 00:28:45,423
Dr. Grant, we're not leaving
this island without our son.

360
00:28:45,882 --> 00:28:47,967
Then you can go and look for him.

361
00:28:48,051 --> 00:28:50,887
Or you can stick with us,
as long as you don't hold us up.

362
00:28:50,970 --> 00:28:52,221
Either way,

363
00:28:53,014 --> 00:28:55,683
you probably won't get off this island alive.

364
00:29:07,362 --> 00:29:09,447
So? What do we do?

365
00:29:10,657 --> 00:29:12,617
We search for your son

366
00:29:14,452 --> 00:29:16,871
in the direction that they're going.

367
00:29:18,331 --> 00:29:19,624
Excellent.

368
00:29:45,400 --> 00:29:46,734
Sorry.

369
00:29:47,652 --> 00:29:50,279
It's nothing you haven't seen before, Paul.

370
00:29:51,990 --> 00:29:54,534
How much weight did you say you lost?

371
00:29:54,617 --> 00:29:57,537
About 25 pounds.
I've been swimming.

372
00:29:58,079 --> 00:30:01,582
Swimming? You hate to swim.
You don't even know how to swim.

373
00:30:01,666 --> 00:30:03,501
I know. I learned.

374
00:30:04,460 --> 00:30:05,586
You look good.

375
00:30:05,712 --> 00:30:06,796
So do you.

376
00:30:11,092 --> 00:30:12,760
How would you classify it, Billy?

377
00:30:12,802 --> 00:30:14,095
Well...

378
00:30:14,721 --> 00:30:16,848
it's a super-predator.

379
00:30:19,017 --> 00:30:20,685
A <i>Suchomimus</i>.

380
00:30:21,269 --> 00:30:23,604
- That snout.
- No, think bigger.

381
00:30:23,855 --> 00:30:26,190
- <i>Baryonyx</i>?
- Not with that sail.

382
00:30:27,775 --> 00:30:29,902
<i>Spinosaurus aegypticus.</i>

383
00:30:32,989 --> 00:30:35,241
I don't remember that on InGen's list.

384
00:30:35,283 --> 00:30:36,659
Because it wasn't on their list.

385
00:30:36,743 --> 00:30:39,662
And it makes you wonder
what else they were up to.

386
00:30:51,549 --> 00:30:53,301
So, Mr. Kirby, tell me.

387
00:30:53,384 --> 00:30:57,930
When you climbed K2, did you base
camp at 25,000 or 30,000 feet?

388
00:30:59,849 --> 00:31:02,602
30,000 feet. We were
pretty close to the top.

389
00:31:02,685 --> 00:31:05,396
You were about 1,000
feet above it, actually.

390
00:31:06,898 --> 00:31:08,816
No, that's a common mistake.

391
00:31:09,859 --> 00:31:13,321
There's no such thing
as Kirby Enterprises, is there?

392
00:31:14,822 --> 00:31:16,532
It's Kirby Paint and Tile Plus.

393
00:31:16,949 --> 00:31:18,826
The "Plus" stands for bathroom fixtures.

394
00:31:18,910 --> 00:31:21,788
We're in the Westgate Shopping
Center, Enid, Oklahoma...

395
00:31:21,829 --> 00:31:24,290
I don't suppose that check
you wrote us is any good.

396
00:31:24,332 --> 00:31:26,626
I will pay you the money I owe you.

397
00:31:26,667 --> 00:31:28,252
This is good.

398
00:31:29,420 --> 00:31:32,799
Here we are, in the worst place
in the world, and we're not being paid!

399
00:31:33,216 --> 00:31:36,969
Wait, fellas. Hold on!
I'll make this up to you.

400
00:31:37,053 --> 00:31:39,972
If you ever do a bathroom or a kitchen...

401
00:31:41,182 --> 00:31:42,975
You're not really a mercenary, are you?

402
00:31:43,017 --> 00:31:44,477
I never said I was.

403
00:31:44,519 --> 00:31:46,771
That's true.
What are you?

404
00:31:47,563 --> 00:31:51,901
I'm like a booking agent. One of
the guys got sick and couldn't come.

405
00:31:52,485 --> 00:31:54,112
Excuse me. Here.

406
00:31:55,029 --> 00:31:57,865
- So you run a hardware store?
- Paint and tile, yeah.

407
00:31:58,533 --> 00:32:00,368
You never can tell about people, can you?

408
00:32:00,451 --> 00:32:01,994
Ain't that the truth?

409
00:32:02,829 --> 00:32:04,664
- Eric!
- Quiet.

410
00:32:04,747 --> 00:32:06,040
Would you stop that?

411
00:32:06,124 --> 00:32:08,543
Dr. Grant says this is very
dangerous territory.

412
00:32:08,584 --> 00:32:12,046
Maybe we should split up.
We could cover twice as much.

413
00:32:12,130 --> 00:32:13,714
Dr. Grant says that's a bad idea.

414
00:32:13,798 --> 00:32:15,341
Dr. Grant says this...

415
00:32:15,383 --> 00:32:18,094
Why hire an expert
if we don't use his advice?

416
00:32:18,177 --> 00:32:21,013
Except Dr. Grant isn't looking for Eric.

417
00:32:21,055 --> 00:32:22,306
He's looking for the coast.

418
00:32:22,390 --> 00:32:23,683
Go ahead and scream.

419
00:32:23,724 --> 00:32:26,894
When that "Tricycloplots" attacks you,
don't come crying to me.

420
00:32:26,936 --> 00:32:27,979
Don't worry about that.

421
00:32:28,062 --> 00:32:30,231
What? What did you say?

422
00:32:30,273 --> 00:32:31,858
What did you say?

423
00:32:31,899 --> 00:32:33,359
Never mind!
Paul, just drop it!

424
00:32:33,401 --> 00:32:36,571
If we split up, I'm going with you guys.

425
00:32:48,166 --> 00:32:49,667
Eric!

426
00:32:50,126 --> 00:32:52,128
Mrs. Kirby!

427
00:32:53,796 --> 00:32:57,466
Mrs. Kirby, the chances are remote
they're still in the vicinity.

428
00:33:03,931 --> 00:33:05,308
Young adult.

429
00:33:07,476 --> 00:33:08,853
Hey, guys!

430
00:33:15,151 --> 00:33:16,944
That's my camera.

431
00:33:21,574 --> 00:33:22,950
The battery's dead.

432
00:33:22,992 --> 00:33:24,911
I've got an idea.

433
00:33:25,786 --> 00:33:27,455
Let me have the camera.

434
00:33:29,874 --> 00:33:32,543
<i>Where were you on that one, Ma?</i>

435
00:33:33,628 --> 00:33:35,922
<i>Okay, dive. Dive.</i>

436
00:33:38,424 --> 00:33:39,675
<i>Come on, Ma.</i>

437
00:33:39,759 --> 00:33:40,801
<i>Eric.</i>

438
00:33:40,885 --> 00:33:42,386
<i>Ma, you were supposed to catch it!</i>

439
00:33:42,803 --> 00:33:45,514
I shot this the morning they disappeared.

440
00:33:45,598 --> 00:33:48,517
<i>No, I want that.
Let me take that.</i>

441
00:33:48,601 --> 00:33:50,811
<i>Come on, give me that.
Come on, baby.</i>

442
00:33:50,937 --> 00:33:54,732
<i>- See anything yet?
- No, not yet!</i>

443
00:33:55,816 --> 00:33:57,360
<i>What was that?</i>

444
00:33:59,028 --> 00:34:02,490
<i>Unclip us. There's a big clip!</i>

445
00:34:02,531 --> 00:34:04,158
<i>I can't see. I can't do it!</i>

446
00:34:06,869 --> 00:34:08,746
<i>I'm going to unhook you, all right?</i>

447
00:34:08,829 --> 00:34:11,624
<i>One, two, three!</i>

448
00:34:12,959 --> 00:34:16,212
<i>Are you okay, buddy?
I'm gonna drop myself down now.</i>

449
00:34:17,296 --> 00:34:18,881
<i>The camera's still on.</i>

450
00:34:21,968 --> 00:34:24,762
He's alive. I know he's alive.

451
00:34:25,054 --> 00:34:26,639
We're gonna find him.

452
00:34:29,308 --> 00:34:31,352
Can you fly one of those?

453
00:34:31,519 --> 00:34:33,062
Maybe.

454
00:34:33,145 --> 00:34:35,231
As long as the sail is not torn.

455
00:34:35,314 --> 00:34:36,983
Well, let's take it.

456
00:34:37,566 --> 00:34:40,361
If we spot a plane, it might be
a good way to get attention.

457
00:34:40,653 --> 00:34:42,154
Pull it over this way.

458
00:34:51,580 --> 00:34:54,333
I got it, hold on!
Okay. Okay.

459
00:34:54,417 --> 00:34:55,918
Oh, my God!

460
00:35:02,508 --> 00:35:04,844
Okay, you're free.

461
00:35:07,388 --> 00:35:09,432
Get her back, Mr. Kirby!

462
00:35:09,515 --> 00:35:11,100
Amanda!

463
00:35:11,642 --> 00:35:12,727
Wait!

464
00:35:23,863 --> 00:35:25,281
Amanda!

465
00:35:33,789 --> 00:35:35,833
Amanda, stop.

466
00:35:37,001 --> 00:35:40,171
I'm sorry. I'm sorry.
I'm sorry about Ben.

467
00:35:43,341 --> 00:35:45,634
It's not Ben, Paul.

468
00:35:46,802 --> 00:35:49,889
It's Eric. He's out there all by himself.

469
00:35:51,223 --> 00:35:53,059
I know, I know.

470
00:35:53,142 --> 00:35:56,395
Our baby is out here all by himself.

471
00:35:56,687 --> 00:35:59,065
Listen to me.
We'll find him.

472
00:35:59,774 --> 00:36:00,941
I promise.

473
00:36:08,532 --> 00:36:09,784
Paul.

474
00:36:13,537 --> 00:36:15,289
Dr. Grant!

475
00:36:16,248 --> 00:36:18,334
You should come look at this!

476
00:36:49,532 --> 00:36:50,866
Raptor.

477
00:37:03,170 --> 00:37:04,755
We're going to find him.

478
00:37:04,839 --> 00:37:07,383
Are you listening to me?
We're going to find him.

479
00:37:07,466 --> 00:37:09,218
Kid's got resources.

480
00:37:09,301 --> 00:37:11,720
Remember what it was like
to try to ground him?

481
00:37:14,181 --> 00:37:15,349
Where's Billy?

482
00:37:21,856 --> 00:37:23,649
What are you doing?

483
00:37:23,816 --> 00:37:25,484
I was photographing the nest.

484
00:37:26,318 --> 00:37:27,653
Don't do that again.

485
00:37:28,529 --> 00:37:29,780
I'm sorry.

486
00:37:31,031 --> 00:37:34,034
If I lose you, it's just me
and the damn tourists.

487
00:37:42,877 --> 00:37:44,503
I bet you Eric is in there.

488
00:37:44,587 --> 00:37:47,131
Don't you think?
I'd bet my bottom dollar!

489
00:37:48,924 --> 00:37:50,593
What do you think it is?

490
00:37:50,676 --> 00:37:54,054
As far as I'm concerned,
it's the Four Seasons.

491
00:38:30,549 --> 00:38:31,800
Eric!

492
00:38:31,884 --> 00:38:33,260
Paul! Don't.

493
00:38:42,770 --> 00:38:44,563
What the hell.

494
00:39:00,496 --> 00:39:03,457
Who's got some change?
It only takes quarters.

495
00:39:03,499 --> 00:39:05,960
I've got $1.10.

496
00:40:01,557 --> 00:40:03,642
This is how you make dinosaurs?

497
00:40:03,976 --> 00:40:05,227
No.

498
00:40:06,270 --> 00:40:08,397
This is how you play God.

499
00:41:04,203 --> 00:41:05,663
This way!

500
00:41:10,834 --> 00:41:12,169
It's locked!

501
00:41:15,923 --> 00:41:17,424
- This way, this way!
- Go! Go!

502
00:41:31,522 --> 00:41:33,607
Hey, over here!

503
00:41:43,992 --> 00:41:45,661
Push, push!

504
00:41:55,295 --> 00:41:56,672
My God.

505
00:41:58,048 --> 00:41:59,800
He's calling for help.

506
00:42:00,300 --> 00:42:01,760
Come on!

507
00:42:24,825 --> 00:42:26,452
Into the herd!

508
00:42:54,521 --> 00:42:56,064
Head for the trees!

509
00:43:24,009 --> 00:43:25,219
Udesky.

510
00:43:44,404 --> 00:43:45,781
Mr. Kirby!

511
00:43:47,449 --> 00:43:48,575
Billy?

512
00:43:48,617 --> 00:43:49,660
Is Alan with you?

513
00:43:57,209 --> 00:43:58,919
Oh, my God.

514
00:43:58,961 --> 00:44:00,587
Mr. Udesky!

515
00:44:03,882 --> 00:44:05,092
He's dead.

516
00:44:07,010 --> 00:44:08,595
Oh, my God.
No, he's not.

517
00:44:08,637 --> 00:44:10,347
No, wait. Something's not right.

518
00:44:10,430 --> 00:44:11,598
We've got to help him.

519
00:44:12,307 --> 00:44:13,517
Amanda!

520
00:44:19,064 --> 00:44:20,148
Hold on!

521
00:44:27,948 --> 00:44:29,283
They set a trap.

522
00:44:30,117 --> 00:44:31,910
They actually set a trap.

523
00:44:37,666 --> 00:44:39,167
What are you saying?

524
00:44:40,043 --> 00:44:41,712
What are you looking for?

525
00:45:53,867 --> 00:45:55,077
Wait!

526
00:46:11,218 --> 00:46:12,386
Thanks.

527
00:46:13,011 --> 00:46:14,513
Thanks a lot, Eric.

528
00:46:17,975 --> 00:46:20,352
- You know who I am?
- Yeah.

529
00:46:21,311 --> 00:46:24,064
Your parents are here.
They're looking for you.

530
00:46:24,898 --> 00:46:25,983
Together?

531
00:46:26,525 --> 00:46:27,609
Together.

532
00:46:29,611 --> 00:46:30,988
That's not good.

533
00:46:31,113 --> 00:46:33,115
They don't do so well together.

534
00:46:33,490 --> 00:46:37,452
You'd be surprised what people
can do when they have to.

535
00:46:52,860 --> 00:46:54,153
You're Alan Grant.

536
00:46:55,738 --> 00:46:57,448
What are you doing here?

537
00:46:59,575 --> 00:47:00,909
Your parents

538
00:47:02,536 --> 00:47:04,079
invited me along.

539
00:47:27,770 --> 00:47:29,521
I read both of your books.

540
00:47:30,981 --> 00:47:32,816
I liked the first one more.

541
00:47:33,108 --> 00:47:35,110
Before you were on the island.

542
00:47:35,944 --> 00:47:37,946
You liked dinosaurs back then.

543
00:47:38,572 --> 00:47:41,158
Back then, they hadn't
tried to eat me yet.

544
00:47:44,703 --> 00:47:47,373
When InGen cleared out,
they left a lot of stuff behind.

545
00:47:47,456 --> 00:47:48,540
Any weapons?

546
00:47:48,624 --> 00:47:51,502
No, and I just used the last
of the gas grenades.

547
00:47:51,877 --> 00:47:53,295
And I appreciate that.

548
00:47:57,132 --> 00:47:59,385
Be careful with that.
T-Rex.

549
00:48:00,177 --> 00:48:01,762
It scares the smaller ones away

550
00:48:01,845 --> 00:48:04,264
but it attracts one really big one with a fin.

551
00:48:05,015 --> 00:48:06,892
This is T-Rex pee?

552
00:48:10,437 --> 00:48:11,855
How did you get it?

553
00:48:11,939 --> 00:48:13,649
You don't want to know.

554
00:48:19,238 --> 00:48:21,031
- Dessert?
- Sure.

555
00:48:26,078 --> 00:48:27,162
I have to tell you,

556
00:48:27,246 --> 00:48:30,374
I'm astonished you've lasted
eight weeks on this island.

557
00:48:32,334 --> 00:48:33,961
Is that all it's been?

558
00:48:35,254 --> 00:48:37,881
You're alive and that's
the important thing.

559
00:48:38,340 --> 00:48:41,260
Thanks to you, that's one
thing we have in common.

560
00:48:43,971 --> 00:48:45,848
Did you read Malcolm's book?

561
00:48:46,890 --> 00:48:48,100
Yeah.

562
00:48:49,518 --> 00:48:50,602
So?

563
00:48:50,978 --> 00:48:52,688
I don't know. I mean...

564
00:48:53,147 --> 00:48:54,857
It was kind of preachy.

565
00:48:55,524 --> 00:48:57,985
And too much chaos.
Everything is chaos.

566
00:48:58,402 --> 00:49:00,821
Seemed like he was high on himself.

567
00:49:03,031 --> 00:49:05,868
That's two things that we have in common.

568
00:49:12,332 --> 00:49:13,500
Listen.

569
00:49:14,209 --> 00:49:15,419
Compys!

570
00:49:40,444 --> 00:49:41,695
Alan!

571
00:49:57,628 --> 00:50:01,048
No matter how this turns out
it wasn't your fault.

572
00:50:02,382 --> 00:50:04,718
Eric's always been a strong-willed kid.

573
00:50:04,843 --> 00:50:06,136
Always.

574
00:50:06,220 --> 00:50:10,307
Then you throw someone like
Ben Hildebrand into the mix, and...

575
00:50:11,558 --> 00:50:12,684
well.

576
00:50:13,685 --> 00:50:14,853
Well, what?

577
00:50:15,813 --> 00:50:18,232
All I'm saying is, it's not your fault.

578
00:50:22,736 --> 00:50:23,862
No.

579
00:50:23,904 --> 00:50:26,698
If he'd been with you,
he'd be completely safe.

580
00:50:27,658 --> 00:50:30,577
You drive five miles
under the speed limit.

581
00:50:31,995 --> 00:50:34,581
And I've totaled three cars in three years.

582
00:50:35,499 --> 00:50:39,044
Well, not three.
The Buick wasn't really totaled.

583
00:50:40,546 --> 00:50:43,590
I just said it was, because
I wanted to get the S.U.V.

584
00:50:48,554 --> 00:50:50,931
I am so sorry that you have to be here.

585
00:50:54,226 --> 00:50:55,269
I'm not.

586
00:52:12,429 --> 00:52:15,349
There's a boat, right alongside the bank.

587
00:52:15,515 --> 00:52:16,600
Looks in good shape.

588
00:52:16,725 --> 00:52:17,809
Rescue boat?

589
00:52:18,185 --> 00:52:19,519
No, no.

590
00:52:20,354 --> 00:52:22,189
Just something left behind.

591
00:52:22,481 --> 00:52:23,690
But it floats.

592
00:52:35,661 --> 00:52:37,204
We stick to the plan.

593
00:52:38,038 --> 00:52:39,498
Head for the coast.

594
00:52:39,539 --> 00:52:41,375
You're not gonna look for Dr. Grant?

595
00:52:41,500 --> 00:52:43,210
The coast was Alan's idea.

596
00:52:43,251 --> 00:52:45,003
If he's alive, that's where he's headed.

597
00:52:45,045 --> 00:52:46,088
I agree.

598
00:52:46,213 --> 00:52:47,547
What about Eric?

599
00:52:48,173 --> 00:52:49,591
Eric's a smart kid.

600
00:52:49,716 --> 00:52:52,219
He probably knows his best
chance is by the coast.

601
00:52:52,344 --> 00:52:55,389
He figures the big dinosaurs
are in the center of the island.

602
00:52:55,430 --> 00:52:56,682
Right?

603
00:52:58,225 --> 00:52:59,393
Sure.

604
00:53:06,191 --> 00:53:08,068
You know what this is?

605
00:53:08,402 --> 00:53:10,445
It's a raptor claw.
I used to have one.

606
00:53:11,196 --> 00:53:12,239
A fossil.

607
00:53:13,740 --> 00:53:14,950
Mine is new.

608
00:53:16,576 --> 00:53:18,954
How much of the island did you explore?

609
00:53:19,454 --> 00:53:21,581
I stayed pretty close to the compound.

610
00:53:21,623 --> 00:53:23,458
I figured if anyone came looking for me,

611
00:53:23,542 --> 00:53:25,252
that's where they'd start.

612
00:53:26,128 --> 00:53:28,422
We need to get to the coast.
That was the plan.

613
00:53:28,463 --> 00:53:29,589
Are you sure?

614
00:53:29,715 --> 00:53:32,426
The closer you get to water,
the bigger things get.

615
00:53:32,467 --> 00:53:35,095
If we can find our way
down into the canyon

616
00:53:35,137 --> 00:53:37,806
we can follow that river out to the coast.

617
00:53:38,598 --> 00:53:40,434
After we find your parents.

618
00:53:40,976 --> 00:53:42,102
Then what?

619
00:53:42,769 --> 00:53:44,646
- One step at a time.
- Listen.

620
00:53:46,398 --> 00:53:48,483
What?

621
00:53:48,650 --> 00:53:50,777
That's my dad's satellite phone!

622
00:53:54,406 --> 00:53:55,907
Wait a minute.
How do you know?

623
00:53:56,825 --> 00:53:59,703
"Kirby Paint and Tile Plus, in Westgate!"

624
00:54:00,454 --> 00:54:01,830
Dad!

625
00:54:16,803 --> 00:54:17,971
Mum!

626
00:54:21,558 --> 00:54:24,394
Mum! Dad!

627
00:54:25,270 --> 00:54:28,190
Eric! This way!

628
00:54:30,942 --> 00:54:33,111
Mum! Dad!

629
00:54:40,619 --> 00:54:43,038
Mum, Dad!
Mum, Dad!

630
00:54:50,045 --> 00:54:52,172
I knew it. My God.

631
00:54:53,131 --> 00:54:54,216
I knew it.

632
00:54:55,300 --> 00:54:58,011
Oh, God. Oh, my God.

633
00:54:58,345 --> 00:55:00,138
Boy, am I glad to see you.

634
00:55:00,597 --> 00:55:02,182
Hey, you got my bag.

635
00:55:02,390 --> 00:55:05,185
Yeah. Lucky strap.

636
00:55:06,520 --> 00:55:08,063
Want me to carry it?

637
00:55:09,689 --> 00:55:11,525
We need to find a gap here.

638
00:55:13,401 --> 00:55:15,862
- How did you know we were here?
- The phone.

639
00:55:15,946 --> 00:55:18,198
That stupid jingle from the store.
I heard it.

640
00:55:18,240 --> 00:55:20,367
- My phone?
- Your satellite phone.

641
00:55:20,450 --> 00:55:22,035
- Where is it?
- I don't have it.

642
00:55:22,119 --> 00:55:23,829
When did you use it last?

643
00:55:24,371 --> 00:55:26,123
On the plane.

644
00:55:26,206 --> 00:55:28,375
I got a call on the plane and...

645
00:55:29,376 --> 00:55:32,629
- What?
- I loaned it to Nash.

646
00:55:33,463 --> 00:55:35,298
He must have had it when he...

647
00:55:45,976 --> 00:55:47,144
Run.

648
00:56:51,541 --> 00:56:53,960
Alan, you want to give me the bag back?

649
00:56:54,461 --> 00:56:55,962
It's okay, I got it.

650
00:56:56,796 --> 00:56:58,506
Please, give me the bag.

651
00:57:02,636 --> 00:57:03,845
It's not safe.

652
00:57:15,565 --> 00:57:16,775
Raptor eggs.

653
00:57:18,818 --> 00:57:21,655
Did you steal raptor eggs?

654
00:57:28,578 --> 00:57:30,330
Now it all makes sense.

655
00:57:30,538 --> 00:57:34,000
I swear, if I'd known you were
going to end up with them...

656
00:57:36,253 --> 00:57:37,379
It was an impulse,

657
00:57:37,462 --> 00:57:38,797
I thought they'd be worth a fortune.

658
00:57:38,838 --> 00:57:41,925
Enough to fund the dig site
another 10 more years.

659
00:57:42,008 --> 00:57:44,886
You have to believe me.
This was a stupid decision,

660
00:57:44,970 --> 00:57:47,138
but I did it with the best intentions.

661
00:57:47,180 --> 00:57:49,307
"With the best intentions."

662
00:57:51,059 --> 00:57:53,853
Some of the worst things imaginable

663
00:57:53,895 --> 00:57:56,273
have been done with the best intentions.

664
00:57:57,357 --> 00:57:58,900
You know what, Billy?

665
00:57:59,276 --> 00:58:04,030
As far as I'm concerned, you're no better
than the people that built this place.

666
00:58:21,339 --> 00:58:22,382
What are you doing?

667
00:58:22,424 --> 00:58:24,592
Those things are after us
because of those.

668
00:58:24,676 --> 00:58:28,221
Those things know we have the eggs.

669
00:58:28,596 --> 00:58:32,684
If I drop them in the river,
they'll still be after us.

670
00:58:33,351 --> 00:58:35,145
What if they catch us with them?

671
00:58:35,228 --> 00:58:37,147
What if they catch us without them?

672
00:58:41,151 --> 00:58:43,445
There's a boat at the bottom,
just downriver.

673
00:58:43,528 --> 00:58:46,239
We can try and make it
to the coast, at least.

674
00:59:41,961 --> 00:59:43,463
- You okay?
- Yeah.

675
00:59:55,016 --> 00:59:57,394
Let's do this one at a time, shall we?

676
01:00:35,890 --> 01:00:37,475
Okay, come on over!

677
01:00:38,435 --> 01:00:39,853
One at a time!

678
01:00:40,061 --> 01:00:41,438
All right, Eric.

679
01:00:41,771 --> 01:00:43,690
I'm going to leave you just for a minute.

680
01:00:43,731 --> 01:00:45,859
And then you'll be right behind me, okay?

681
01:00:45,942 --> 01:00:47,068
Mum!

682
01:00:47,610 --> 01:00:50,155
I've been alone in a water
truck for eight weeks.

683
01:00:50,238 --> 01:00:53,366
I think I can manage the next
two minutes without you.

684
01:00:56,578 --> 01:00:58,580
Right, we're all together now.

685
01:01:24,981 --> 01:01:26,149
Okay, Eric.

686
01:01:26,441 --> 01:01:27,901
- Okay.
- Come on, honey!

687
01:01:27,984 --> 01:01:29,194
Over you go.

688
01:02:03,102 --> 01:02:04,354
Mum?

689
01:02:06,814 --> 01:02:08,066
Oh, my God.

690
01:02:08,525 --> 01:02:09,776
What is it?

691
01:02:10,401 --> 01:02:12,111
It's a birdcage.

692
01:02:12,779 --> 01:02:13,905
For what?

693
01:02:20,453 --> 01:02:21,663
Dad!

694
01:02:21,746 --> 01:02:22,872
Eric!

695
01:02:39,889 --> 01:02:41,516
I can't see him!

696
01:02:50,483 --> 01:02:51,568
No!

697
01:02:59,784 --> 01:03:00,952
This way!

698
01:03:16,843 --> 01:03:18,094
Billy, stop!

699
01:03:18,803 --> 01:03:21,306
Billy, stop!
Don't, Billy!

700
01:03:40,992 --> 01:03:42,535
Eric, hold on!

701
01:03:47,790 --> 01:03:48,875
Eric!

702
01:04:02,180 --> 01:04:03,222
Jump!

703
01:04:30,083 --> 01:04:31,250
Billy!

704
01:04:44,055 --> 01:04:45,223
Let go now!

705
01:05:31,394 --> 01:05:32,812
- Eric!
- He's there.

706
01:05:45,616 --> 01:05:48,244
There he is! He's across the river!

707
01:05:48,745 --> 01:05:50,288
Get him out of here!

708
01:05:52,790 --> 01:05:54,459
- No!
- Go, Eric!

709
01:05:54,834 --> 01:05:56,294
What about Billy?

710
01:06:11,309 --> 01:06:12,560
Hang on, Billy!

711
01:06:21,152 --> 01:06:22,320
Wait.

712
01:06:26,199 --> 01:06:27,325
Get away!

713
01:07:52,577 --> 01:07:54,412
There's plenty of gasoline.

714
01:07:54,620 --> 01:07:57,498
Now we've just got to make
our way to the coast.

715
01:07:57,957 --> 01:08:00,459
Work out some sort of a signal.

716
01:08:01,085 --> 01:08:04,630
Build a fire or something.
Something they can see from the air.

717
01:08:10,678 --> 01:08:11,804
Dr. Grant?

718
01:08:13,639 --> 01:08:15,600
Hey, Eric, how are you doing?

719
01:08:25,526 --> 01:08:27,153
I'm sorry about Billy.

720
01:08:31,032 --> 01:08:33,826
You know what the last thing
I said to him was?

721
01:08:34,285 --> 01:08:35,620
I said,

722
01:08:36,037 --> 01:08:39,040
"You're as bad as the people
that built this place."

723
01:08:39,498 --> 01:08:44,587
Which wasn't true. Billy was just young.
That's all.

724
01:08:46,631 --> 01:08:49,550
I have a theory that there
are two kinds of boys.

725
01:08:49,634 --> 01:08:51,802
There are those who want
to be astronomers

726
01:08:51,844 --> 01:08:54,305
and those who want to be astronauts.

727
01:08:54,847 --> 01:08:57,767
The astronomer, or the paleontologist

728
01:08:57,850 --> 01:09:01,479
gets to study these amazing things

729
01:09:01,520 --> 01:09:03,481
from a place of complete safety.

730
01:09:04,190 --> 01:09:06,359
But then you never get to go into space.

731
01:09:06,400 --> 01:09:07,735
Exactly.

732
01:09:08,194 --> 01:09:11,197
It's the difference between
imagining and seeing.

733
01:09:11,280 --> 01:09:13,824
To be able to touch them.

734
01:09:14,408 --> 01:09:18,579
And that's all Billy wanted.

735
01:09:25,711 --> 01:09:26,837
Dr. Grant.

736
01:09:55,950 --> 01:09:57,952
You know something, Dr. Grant?

737
01:09:59,745 --> 01:10:01,122
Billy was right.

738
01:10:27,106 --> 01:10:28,274
Listen.

739
01:10:49,170 --> 01:10:50,880
Find it before it stops ringing!

740
01:10:50,963 --> 01:10:52,882
Eric, you stay on that boat.

741
01:10:55,843 --> 01:10:58,054
It's here. It's here.

742
01:10:59,555 --> 01:11:00,806
God.

743
01:11:07,813 --> 01:11:08,981
I got it.

744
01:11:17,198 --> 01:11:18,657
Hello? Hello?

745
01:11:18,699 --> 01:11:22,244
<i>You, too, can own a timeshare
in beautiful Guadalajara.</i>

746
01:11:22,328 --> 01:11:25,206
<i>Enjoy a meal in one of our
four-star restaurants.</i>

747
01:11:25,331 --> 01:11:26,373
Look out!

748
01:11:46,435 --> 01:11:48,354
Turn off the power.

749
01:11:48,395 --> 01:11:50,356
You've got juice for maybe one call.

750
01:11:50,397 --> 01:11:52,691
Whatever you do,
don't call the U.S. Embassy.

751
01:11:52,733 --> 01:11:54,568
They won't do a damn thing.

752
01:11:54,860 --> 01:11:57,404
Hey, guys, come here!
Look at this!

753
01:12:04,078 --> 01:12:05,538
Bonitos.

754
01:12:05,704 --> 01:12:07,206
Something must have spooked them!

755
01:12:07,790 --> 01:12:09,875
Get the engine going, Mr. Kirby.

756
01:12:17,383 --> 01:12:18,884
Open the throttle!

757
01:12:25,558 --> 01:12:26,642
Pick up.

758
01:12:32,106 --> 01:12:33,149
Hello?

759
01:12:33,399 --> 01:12:34,441
Ellie?

760
01:12:34,900 --> 01:12:36,068
Hello?

761
01:12:36,443 --> 01:12:37,486
Charlie!

762
01:12:38,445 --> 01:12:40,072
- Charlie!
- <i>Hello?</i>

763
01:12:40,948 --> 01:12:43,909
Charlie, take the phone to Mummy now!

764
01:12:44,785 --> 01:12:46,495
<i>Take the phone to Mummy!</i>

765
01:12:46,620 --> 01:12:48,789
It's the dinosaur man!

766
01:12:49,582 --> 01:12:50,583
Okay.

767
01:12:50,749 --> 01:12:51,792
<i>Listen to me, Charlie.</i>

768
01:12:52,960 --> 01:12:54,003
Charlie!

769
01:12:55,087 --> 01:12:58,132
Are you taking the phone to Mum? Charlie!

770
01:13:27,536 --> 01:13:28,871
<i>Hi, everybody!</i>

771
01:13:38,631 --> 01:13:40,299
<i>It's great to be here!</i>

772
01:13:40,341 --> 01:13:42,718
<i>Hi, there! How are you?</i>

773
01:13:52,311 --> 01:13:54,146
<i>This is going to be great.</i>

774
01:14:06,992 --> 01:14:09,245
- Who's on the phone?
- The dinosaur man.

775
01:14:09,328 --> 01:14:10,955
He is? Alan?

776
01:14:11,247 --> 01:14:14,208
Look in the boxes!
Find a weapon!

777
01:14:21,298 --> 01:14:23,175
Where's the phone?

778
01:14:23,259 --> 01:14:24,885
- There!
- Get the phone!

779
01:14:34,687 --> 01:14:36,230
Ellie, listen to me!

780
01:14:36,522 --> 01:14:38,774
Are you on a cell phone?
I can't hear you.

781
01:14:38,857 --> 01:14:42,027
The river! Site B!
The river!

782
01:14:44,863 --> 01:14:46,115
Hello?

783
01:16:52,324 --> 01:16:55,244
Dad! Dad!

784
01:17:13,512 --> 01:17:14,847
Dad!

785
01:17:19,184 --> 01:17:21,186
Paul, you jerk!

786
01:17:23,730 --> 01:17:25,691
You can't leave me like this!

787
01:17:28,360 --> 01:17:30,028
I'm not going anywhere!

788
01:17:46,545 --> 01:17:49,631
Remember when we went fishing
a couple of summers ago?

789
01:17:50,466 --> 01:17:53,760
I was putting the boat
in the water and the trailer sank?

790
01:17:54,720 --> 01:17:58,265
The tow truck tried to pull us out
and it got dragged in, too.

791
01:17:59,224 --> 01:18:01,935
The truck driver wanted
to knock my lights out.

792
01:18:07,566 --> 01:18:08,901
I miss fishing.

793
01:18:14,740 --> 01:18:15,908
Mr. Kirby?

794
01:18:20,913 --> 01:18:23,582
That lady you called.
Who is she?

795
01:18:24,500 --> 01:18:26,585
How do you know that she can help us?

796
01:18:26,668 --> 01:18:28,670
She's the one person
I could always count on.

797
01:18:28,754 --> 01:18:29,963
I owe her a lot.

798
01:18:31,089 --> 01:18:33,008
I don't think I ever told her that.

799
01:18:33,091 --> 01:18:34,134
You should.

800
01:18:37,137 --> 01:18:38,430
You hear that?

801
01:18:40,098 --> 01:18:41,266
The ocean.

802
01:18:57,333 --> 01:18:58,835
They want the eggs.

803
01:18:59,961 --> 01:19:02,088
Otherwise, we'd be dead already.

804
01:19:07,260 --> 01:19:10,513
Everybody get down.
She's challenging us.

805
01:19:29,949 --> 01:19:31,909
She thinks you stole the eggs.

806
01:19:31,951 --> 01:19:33,119
Get behind me.

807
01:19:53,472 --> 01:19:56,475
Give me the eggs.

808
01:20:17,330 --> 01:20:18,497
Do it, Mum.

809
01:20:37,350 --> 01:20:39,185
No. Call for help.

810
01:21:26,065 --> 01:21:27,566
That's a helicopter.

811
01:21:37,243 --> 01:21:40,329
<i>Dr. Grant? Dr. Alan Grant?</i>

812
01:21:40,413 --> 01:21:41,914
Stop!

813
01:21:41,956 --> 01:21:44,500
That's a very bad idea!

814
01:21:53,092 --> 01:21:55,011
- Second Squad, move!
- Wow!

815
01:21:56,053 --> 01:21:59,682
You have to thank her now.
She sent the Navy and the Marines.

816
01:22:01,434 --> 01:22:03,185
God bless you, Ellie.

817
01:22:15,197 --> 01:22:17,783
- Dr. Grant, is this man with you?
- What?

818
01:22:27,877 --> 01:22:29,128
You made it.

819
01:22:30,671 --> 01:22:31,881
Yeah.

820
01:22:32,840 --> 01:22:34,467
I rescued your hat.

821
01:22:35,468 --> 01:22:37,178
Dr. Grant, we need to go now!

822
01:22:37,261 --> 01:22:38,554
Oh, yeah.

823
01:22:40,890 --> 01:22:43,059
Well, that's the important thing.

824
01:22:43,642 --> 01:22:44,727
Dr. Grant!

825
01:22:47,313 --> 01:22:49,982
Can I get you to sit here
and buckle up, sir?

826
01:22:56,864 --> 01:23:00,409
<i>Sea Shadow to Dust Storm,
six souls on board.</i>

827
01:23:02,161 --> 01:23:04,246
<i>Dust Storm to Sea Shadow, roger.</i>

828
01:23:08,918 --> 01:23:10,544
What the hell is that?

829
01:23:11,837 --> 01:23:13,422
Dr. Grant, look!

830
01:23:23,349 --> 01:23:25,351
Where do you think they're going?

831
01:23:26,227 --> 01:23:29,313
I don't know. Maybe just looking
for new nesting grounds.

832
01:23:29,355 --> 01:23:31,357
It's a whole new world for them.

833
01:23:31,440 --> 01:23:33,859
I dare them to nest in Enid, Oklahoma.

834
01:23:40,683 --> 01:23:41,934
Let's go home.